<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stele of the Vultures</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/25th-century-bce">25th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Stele of the Vultures is a fragmentary limestone monument erected around 2450 BCE by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, to celebrate his victory over the neighbouring city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a>. It shows the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a> holding Lagash's enemies in a net and Eannatum leading a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> of soldiers, while vultures carry off the dead. Found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a> (Tello), it is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> and is one of the earliest known war monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures.jpg" alt="Stele of the Vultures" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Background: Kikuyu3 Elements: Eric Gaba (Sting) Composite: पाटलिपुत्र 10:52, 30 April 2020 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stele_of_the_Vultures_in_the_Louvre_Museum_(enhanced_composite).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Only seven fragments of the stele are known. The French archaeologist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernest-de-sarzec">Ernest de Sarzec</a> found the first three during excavations at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Tello</a> in the early 1880s, and three more came to light in the excavations of 1888–1889. A seventh fragment, thought to have come from Tello, was bought on the antiquities market by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> in 1898. The museum twice refused to hand it over, but gave it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> in 1932 so that it could be set into the reconstructed stele. F. Thureau-Dangin first translated the text in 1907.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>As reconstructed, the monument would have stood 1.80 metres high, 1.30 metres wide and 11 centimetres thick, with a rounded top and reliefs carved on both faces of a single limestone slab. It belongs to a tradition of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in the middle and later third millennium BCE in which military victories were commemorated on stone, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victory-stele-of-naram-sin">Victory Stele of Naram-Sin</a> of the following Akkadian period is a comparable work.</p><p>One face is read as mythological. Its upper register shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>, identified by the lion-headed eagle in his left hand, beside a net full of naked bodies, with the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninhursag">Ninhursag</a> behind him; the badly damaged lower register may have shown the god on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> drawn by mythical animals. The other face, read as historical, has four registers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a> leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> over trampled enemies while vultures carry off severed heads, the king in a chariot ahead of marching spearmen, a priest pouring a libation beside a heap of bodies being buried by workers with baskets, and a hand holding a spear to an enemy's head. Some Sumerologists reconstruct a caption there as Kalbum, King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Sumerian cuneiform inscription fills the spaces between the figures and runs from one side to the other. Of roughly 840 lines, 350 survive complete and 130 in part. In it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eannatum">Eannatum</a> describes a dispute with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umma">Umma</a> over Gu-Edin, a tract of farmland between the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, which ended in a battle won by Eannatum, called the beloved of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ningirsu">Ningirsu</a>. After the battle the leader of Umma swore never again to cross into the territory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a>, on pain of divine punishment. The scholar Barton argued that the story of the Garden of Eden may draw on the stele.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stele of the Vultures” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sumerian warfare had relied heavily on close-order infantry, as depicted on the Standard of Ur and the <b>Stele of the Vultures</b>; Sargon's army appears to have made greater use of lightly equipped troops and archers, which allowed more flexible tactics.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Stele of the Vultures</b> shows Eannatum, inspired by Ningirsu, dismount from his chariot and lead his men on foot; with spears lowered the army of Lagash advanced in a dense phalanx and after a brief clash won, one of the earliest organised battles known.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lagash">Lagash · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Stele of the Vultures</b>, raised about 2450 BCE by Eannatum of Lagash, celebrates his victory over neighbouring Umma in a long dispute over the land between them, and around 2350 BCE Urukagina of Lagash proclaimed reforms against the exactions of officials and priests.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The war with Lagash is told from the Lagash side, notably on Eannatum's <b>Stele of the Vultures</b> and in the inscriptions of Enmetena, which describe Umma breaking treaties and seizing land.</blockquote><a href="/state/umma">Umma</a></li></ul></section>
